2. Microsoft's Azure SDKs and tools (MIT, Copyright Microsoft). Microsoft publishes its SDKs under the MIT licence. The
   following were written after reading their source, and in places follow it closely:
   - Event Hubs partition keys (crates/services/eventhubs/src/model.rs: `partition_for_key`, `hash_key`, `lookup3`): the hash
     that chooses a partition from a partition key is the function of `hashPartitionKey` / `computeHash` in `@azure/event-hubs`
     (JavaScript), `PartitionResolver` in Azure.Messaging.EventHubs (.NET) and `azure_messaging_eventhubs` (Rust). It is Bob
     Jenkins' lookup3 (`hashlittle2`, public domain); the mixing steps are those of lookup3.
   - Cosmos DB effective partition keys (crates/services/cosmos/src/epk.rs): MurmurHash3 (Austin Appleby, public domain), used
     as the Cosmos SDKs use it to turn a partition key into a position in the key space.
   - Cosmos DB batch rows (crates/services/cosmos/src/hybridrow.rs): HybridRow is Microsoft's row format. The two schemas it
     reads and writes (`BatchOperation`, `BatchResult`) are those of `Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos/src/Batch/HybridRowBatchSchemas.json`
     in Azure/azure-cosmos-dotnet-v3. The tests hold request bytes that Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos 3.64.0 (its HybridRow library) sent
     while this project's own tests ran.
   - Cosmos DB resource ids (crates/services/cosmos/src/rid.rs): the byte layout of `_rid` values, which the Java and .NET SDKs
     parse (`ResourceId`), worked out from how those SDKs read them. No code was copied.
   - The Azure Functions Blob extension (Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs.Extensions.Storage.Blobs, source in Azure/azure-sdk-for-net,
     MIT) was read, in its public source, to see which Blob and Storage Analytics requests it makes (`$logs` format and the
     container scan). No code of it is in this repository.
   - Test inputs: the six SDK test suites (tests/sdk-*) were written from the SDKs' documentation and from the expectations of
     Microsoft's live tests, not copied from them. Searched 2026-11-13 against the upstream test directories of the Python, Go,
     JavaScript, .NET and Java SDKs: no test is a copy; a few small inputs mirror an upstream test because the recording they
     are compared with was made by that test (for example the certificate policy of `test_policy` in
     tests/sdk-python/keyvault/test_kv_certificates_basic.py).

