4. Recordings of real Azure traffic and REST specifications (MIT, Copyright Microsoft). Error message texts, response
   shapes, header values and a few account property defaults (for example crates/services/blob/src/errors.rs and
   crates/services/servicebus/src/admin/error.rs, and short bodies in tests) were taken from recordings made by the Azure SDK
   test suites, which Microsoft publishes in Azure/azure-sdk-assets (MIT), and from the REST API specifications in
   Azure/azure-rest-api-specs (MIT). These are short strings and values that a client needs to see to behave as it does against
   Azure; they are reproduced as facts about a service's behaviour. Larger things that come from the recordings:
   - crates/services/blob/src/query/avro_schema.json and crates/services/blob/src/query/testdata/*: the Avro schema of
     "quick query" answers and answers of the real service, decoded from the recordings, as test data.
   - crates/services/blob/src/query/avro.rs and the Storage Analytics log format (crates/services/blob/src/analytics.rs) follow
     Microsoft's documentation ("Storage Analytics log format", "Storage Analytics logged operations and status messages").
   The recordings and the specifications themselves are not in this repository (`refs/` is not shipped; `tools/fetch-refs.sh`
   fetches them from Microsoft's repositories for the replay tool). Only scores computed from them are here
   (fidelity/SCORES.md).

5. Azurite and the development accounts. The development storage account `devstoreaccount1` and its key, and the key of the Cosmos DB
   emulator, are published by Microsoft for local development and are used here as constants (Azurite, MIT, Copyright Microsoft).
   crates/core/src/storage/sig/vectors.rs holds signature vectors made with the Azure SDK for Python, with the signing code of
   Azurite for the older API versions, and by hand from the REST documentation; no Azurite code is in this repository.

6. Specifications. The AMQP 1.0 types and framing (crates/amqp) follow the OASIS AMQP 1.0 standard; the WebSocket framing
   (crates/core/src/websocket.rs) follows RFC 6455; HTTP, TLS, X.509 and DER, the cryptography that Key Vault offers, and the OData
   and SQL dialects of the services follow their public specifications and documentation. A standard is a specification, not a
   work copied into the repository. The AMQP, WebSocket, DER and SQL code was written for azemu; on 2026-11-13 it was compared,
   five lines at a time, with every crate in the Cargo registry of the build machine (666 crates, among them fe2o3-amqp,
   rcgen, der, asn1-rs, boa_parser, the Azure SDK for Rust crates) and with tungstenite, fastwebsockets, soketto, sqlparser,
   apache-avro, kafka-protocol, rskafka, yasna and others: nothing was found beyond lookup3 (above), the field list of a Kafka record batch (whose order the protocol fixes) and short
   boilerplate.

